Problem Statement: Check if the i-th Bit is Set
Description: Given a number n and an integer i, 
Write a program to check whether the i-th bit from the right (0-indexed, where the Least Significant Bit is at index 0) in the binary representation of n is set to 1 or 0.
You must provide two approaches: 
Left Shift Approach: Using the bitwise left shift operator (<<).
Right Shift Approach: Using the bitwise right shift operator (>>).

Using the Left Shift (<<):
In this we create a mask. We take the number 1 (which has only its 0-th bit set) and shift it left by i positions.
This creates a binary mask where only the i-th bits is 1.
When we perform a bitwise AND (&) between the mask and our original number n, all other bits are cleared to 0. If the i-th bit in n was a 1, the result will be non-zero.

bool isBitSetLeftShift(int n, int i) {
    //Create a mask by shifting 1 to the left by i positions 
    int mask = 1 << i;

    //Perform bitwise AND. If the result is non-zero the bit is set 
    if ((n & mask) != 0) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

int main() {
    int n = 13; // Binary: 1101
    int i = 2;  // We want to check the bit at index 2 (which is 1)
    
    cout << "Using Left Shift:\n";
    if (isBitSetLeftShift(n, i)) {
        cout << "The " << i << "-th bit of " << n << " is SET (1).\n";
    } else {
        cout << "The " << i << "-th bit of " << n << " is NOT SET (0).\n";
    }
    
    return 0;
}

TC: O(1)
SC: O(1)

Approach 2: Using Right Shift (>>) 
Instead of moving the 1 to align with the bit we want to check, we move the target bit to align with 1. 
By shifting the number n to the right by i positions the i-th bit becomes the Least Significant Bit (the rightmost bit).
We then perform a bitwise AND (&) with 1. 
If the result is 1 the bit was set.

bool isBitSetRightShift(int n, int i) {
    //Shift the number n to the right by i positions 
    int shifted_n = n >> i;

    //Perform bitwise AND with 1. If it equals 1, bit is set 
    if ((shifted_n & 1) == 1) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

int main() {
    int n = 13; // Binary: 1101
    int i = 1;  // We want to check the bit at index 1 (which is 0)
    
    cout << "Using Right Shift:\n";
    if (isBitSetRightShift(n, i)) {
        cout << "The " << i << "-th bit of " << n << " is SET (1).\n";
    } else {
        cout << "The " << i << "-th bit of " << n << " is NOT SET (0).\n";
    }
    
    return 0;
}

TC: O(1)
SC: O(1)

